```javascript
function reverseAdjectiveOrder(str) {
  // Split the string into an array of words based on spaces
  const words = str.split(" ");

  // Filter out common stop words (a, an, the, etc.)
  const filteredWords = words.filter((word) => !STOP_WORDS.includes(word));

  // Reverse the order of the remaining words
  const reversedWords = filteredWords.reverse();

  // Join the reversed words back into a string with spaces between them
  return reversedWords.join(" ") + " ";
}

// Define an array of common stop words
const STOP_WORDS = ["a", "an", "the"];

// Example usage
console.log(reverseAdjectiveOrder("Beautiful green trees are in the park"));
// Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

In this implementation, we first split the input string into an array of words using `split()`. We then filter out any common stop words using `filter()`, which allows us to avoid reversing these words since they don't typically modify the meaning of a sentence. Finally, we reverse the order of the remaining words using `reverse()` and join them back together into a single string using `join()`. The resulting string is returned by the function.

