Unfortunately, JSON is a text-based data interchange format used for transmitting data between a server and web applications as requested by users. It does not have built-in sorting functionality.

To sort a JSON object based on its key values, you need to convert the JSON object into a JavaScript object, sort the keys using the `Array.prototype.sort()` method, and then convert it back into JSON format.

Here's an implementation of this approach:

```js
function sortJsonByKeys(json) {
  const obj = JSON.parse(json);
  const keys = Object.keys(obj).sort();
  const sortedObj = {};

  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length; i++) {
    sortedObj[keys[i]] = obj[keys[i]];
  }

  return JSON.stringify(sortedObj);
}
```

This function takes a JSON string as input, parses it into a JavaScript object, sorts the keys, creates a new object with the sorted keys, and returns the sorted object in JSON format. You can use this function like this:

```js
const jsonString = '{"name": "John", "age": 25, "city": "New York"}';
const sortedJsonString = sortJsonByKeys(jsonString);
console.log(sortedJsonString); // Output: {"age": 25, "city": "New York", "name": "John"}
```

Note that this implementation assumes that all key values are unique. If your JSON object contains duplicate keys, they will be sorted arbitrarily. To avoid this issue, you may want to modify the `sort()` method to handle duplicate keys appropriately.

