To make the circle element in your CSS code look more 3D, you can apply the following changes:

1. Add depth to the circle by using a radial gradient for the fill property. This will create a circular gradient that gives the circle a sense of volume. Here's an example:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(at center, #ffffff 20%, #f2f2f2 20%) repeat scroll 0% 0% / 150% 150%;
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75));
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #ffffff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

2. Use the `transform` property with a perspective and translateZ values to give the circle some perspective and move it forward. Here's an example:

```css
.circle {
  ...
  transform: translateZ(10px) rotateX(-5deg) rotateY(5deg);
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
}
```

3. Increase the contrast between the circle and its surroundings to make it stand out more. You can do this by adjusting the colors used in the CSS properties. For instance, you can change the color of the grid lines and axes to have less contrast with the circle. Here's an example:

```css
.grid {
  stroke: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
}

.xaxis, .yaxis {
  stroke: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
}
```

Combining these changes should result in a more 3D-looking circle element. Feel free to experiment with different values and find what works best for your design.

